# LEADING SLASHES ARE LOAD-BEARING.
#
# A bare `photos/` matches a directory of that name at ANY depth. It silently
# excluded src/photos/ — actual source code — from every commit, so Railway
# crashed on "Cannot find module '../photos/index.ts'" while local builds worked
# fine because the file was only ever on disk. Anchor anything meant to be
# root-only.

node_modules/
.DS_Store

# Secrets
.env
.env.*

# Runtime data — root only, never src/
/photos/
/*.db
/*.db-shm
/*.db-wal
/*.sqlite
/*.sqlite3

# Build output — root only
/dist/
/web/dist/

# Archive snapshots — root only, never committed (they hold the comps archive)
/backups/

# scratch verification scripts
/check.mjs
/poll.mjs
/v.mjs
/shot.mjs

# machine-specific: generated by the scheduled-task setup, holds an absolute bun path
/scripts/daily.cmd
/logs/

# UI verification screenshots. `src/browser/ui-verify.mjs` writes ~7.6 MB per
# run and the point of them is COMPARING two runs on one machine, not history —
# six audit phases would put 45 MB of near-identical PNGs in the repo forever.
# The assertions are the durable artefact; they live in the script.
/docs/ui/

# Ad-hoc screenshot dumps. `docs/shots/` and `docs/now-*.png` were committed as
# handoff artifacts and then went stale in place, showing a product state that
# no longer existed. The audit called them "misleading handoff artifacts" and it
# was right: a screenshot in git is a claim about the present that nothing keeps
# true. `src/browser/ui-verify.mjs` writes fresh ones on demand.
/docs/shots/
/docs/now-*.png
/mc-sample.txt
